# Fiber Core Concepts & Performance

## Zero-Allocation Philosophy

Fiber is built on Fasthttp with a **zero-allocation design**. Values returned from `fiber.Ctx` are **NOT immutable** and are **reused across requests** for maximum performance.

---

## Context Value Immutability

### Critical Rule

**⚠️ Context values are ONLY valid within the handler scope**

Variables obtained from context (`Params()`, `Query()`, `Body()`, `Get()`, `Cookies()`) will be **reused** when the handler returns. Storing references beyond handler scope causes **data corruption**.

### ❌ INCORRECT - Data Corruption Risk

```go
var globalUsername string // DON'T: Will be corrupted!

app.Get("/user/:username", func(c *fiber.Ctx) error {
    // ❌ Storing reference to context value globally
    globalUsername = c.Params("username")
    return c.SendString("Hello")
})

// Later, in another request:
// globalUsername now contains data from a DIFFERENT request!
```

```go
app.Get("/async/:data", func(c *fiber.Ctx) error {
    data := c.Params("data")

    go func() {
        // ❌ DANGER: data is reused after handler returns
        time.Sleep(1 * time.Second)
        processData(data) // May contain wrong data!
    }()

    return c.SendString("Processing")
})
```

### ✅ CORRECT - Safe Value Persistence

```go
import "github.com/gofiber/utils"

app.Get("/user/:username", func(c *fiber.Ctx) error {
    username := c.Params("username")

    // ✅ Option 1: Use utils.CopyString (recommended)
    usernameCopy := utils.CopyString(username)

    // Now safe to use anywhere
    go processUser(usernameCopy)
    storeInCache(usernameCopy)

    return c.SendString("Hello " + usernameCopy)
})
```

```go
app.Post("/data", func(c *fiber.Ctx) error {
    bodyBytes := c.Body()

    // ✅ Option 2: Manual buffer copy
    buffer := make([]byte, len(bodyBytes))
    copy(buffer, bodyBytes)
    bodyCopy := buffer

    go processAsync(bodyCopy) // Safe!

    return c.SendStatus(200)
})
```

```go
app.Get("/query", func(c *fiber.Ctx) error {
    search := c.Query("q")

    // ✅ Option 3: String conversion (works but less explicit)
    searchCopy := string([]byte(search))

    return c.JSON(fiber.Map{
        "query": searchCopy,
    })
})
```

